course: ai
lesson: 18
title: Cars, doctors, scientists and translators
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: AI already works quietly in lots of grown-up jobs: it translates letters, reads menus aloud, writes captions, sorts emails and carries plates in some restaurants.
explain: Picture Grandad holding his phone over a menu he cannot read; the phone reads it out, and suddenly he can choose his own dinner again.
explain: But every one of these helpers is a guesser: captions can turn a word into "duck", a translation can twist an important letter, and a quiz made by AI can have a wrong answer.
explain: So the good habit is the same at school, at work and at home: use the helper, then check the parts that matter.
fact: Music apps learn from your listening habits and build playlists of songs like the ones you already play.
fact: A playlist made only from your old habits rarely brings you something truly new.
fact: Translation tools help families who speak other languages understand letters, but they can make mistakes.
fact: Important translations should be checked by a person who speaks both languages.
fact: AI tools help people with disabilities, for example by reading text aloud, describing pictures and writing captions.
fact: Automatic captions guess words from sound and sometimes mishear, which matters a lot for deaf viewers.
fact: Automated phone calls, or robocalls, use recorded or AI voices; some are surveys and some are scams.
fact: "People like you also bought" lists are made to help shops sell you more.
fact: Photo apps can recognise the same face across many photos and group them together; that means faces are stored somewhere, which is a privacy worry.
fact: Teachers and other workers use AI tools to save time, and they need to check the results.
fact: AI can sort emails at work and save time, but it sometimes puts important ones in the wrong pile.
fact: Some restaurants use simple robots, guided by sensors, to carry food to tables.
exercise: Lily's music app makes a playlist "just for her". How does it know what she likes? | It learned from what she listens to | Her friends secretly told the app | It reads her mind through the headphones | It picks songs at random and gets lucky
exercise: What might Lily miss if she only uses the app's playlists? | New kinds of songs she has never tried | Songs she already loves the most | Every song made before she was born | The volume button on her phone
exercise: Connor's school uses AI to translate letters for families. What is the wise extra step? | Check important letters with someone who speaks both languages | Send each letter through the tool twice | Only translate the letters that are short, because long ones always go wrong | Let the families guess the hard words
exercise: Chloe's grandad uses his phone to read menus aloud. What does this show? | AI can help people with weak eyes or other disabilities | Phones are better at eating out in restaurants than people are | Grandad has forgotten how to read | Menus are now written only for phones
exercise: Automatic captions say "duck" instead of a very different word. Who could this really matter for? | Deaf viewers who rely on the captions | People who are watching with the sound on | People who only look at the pictures | Nobody, captions are never important
exercise: Why do automatic captions sometimes mishear? | They guess words from sounds, like voice assistants | Someone types them in a hurry | They are written before the video is made | They translate every word into another language
exercise: Jake's mum gets a "robot" phone call asking survey questions. What is it? | An automated call using a recorded or AI voice | A real person who talks very slowly | A message from a robot that lives nearby | A call that every phone must answer
exercise: If a robocall seems untrustworthy, what should Jake's mum do? | Hang up | Answer every question to be polite | Give her card number to stop the calls | Call it back and argue with it
exercise: Mia's shopping app says "people like you also bought...". What is it for? | Getting her to buy more things | Warning her about bad products | Telling her what she really needs | Saving her money on everything
exercise: Before buying from that list, what should Mia ask herself? | Do I really need this? | Is it the most expensive one? | Did a robot choose it? | How many people bought it?
exercise: Sam's photo app groups all the photos of Grandad together. How? | It recognises the same face across many photos | Grandad labels each photo himself | The app asks Grandad's friends who he is | It sorts photos by the colour of jumpers
exercise: What sensible worry comes with face-grouping photo apps? | Privacy: whose face is stored, and where | The photos might turn black and white | Grandad's face might change in real life | The app might delete all the photos of cats
exercise: Lily's teacher makes a quiz with AI and finds a wrong answer. What does this show? | Grown-ups use AI too, and need to check it | Teachers should never make quizzes again | The wrong answer must be right after all | AI quizzes are always perfect
exercise: Connor's dad says AI sorts his emails and saves an hour a day. What should he still do? | Check what it sorts, so important emails aren't lost | Stop reading emails altogether | Let the AI answer all his emails too, so he never has to read them | Delete everything the AI puts aside
exercise: Chloe sees a robot carrying plates in a restaurant. What guides it? | Sensors that help it find its way | A waiter with a remote control | The smell of the food on the plates | Music that tells it where to go
exercise: In the robot restaurant, who should Chloe still thank? | The people who cooked and served | Only the robot that brought the plate | Nobody, robots did all the work | The company that built the tables
test: "translator app did my nan's letter from the council, its fine yeah?" Best reply? | For something important, get a person who speaks both languages to check | Yes, translator apps never make mistakes | No, translator apps only work for short words, not for whole letters | Yes, councils write in robot language anyway
test: Your friend laughs at funny captions: "who cares, it's just a joke". What is a fair answer? | It's funny, but deaf viewers rely on captions to understand | Captions are only there to make people laugh, so mistakes make them better | Nobody reads captions, so it never matters | Captions are always right, so it can't happen
test: What would happen if the email sorter put the boss's urgent email in the junk pile and nobody checked? | Something important could be missed | The email would move itself back | The boss would never send emails again | The sorter would apologise to the boss
test: In a cartoon a robot waiter cooks, serves, chats and comforts a crying child. What is closer to real life now? | Real restaurant robots mostly carry plates, guided by sensors | Real robot waiters already do all of that in most restaurants today | Real restaurants are run entirely by robots | Real robots cook but never carry plates
test: Why does Lily's "just for her" playlist sound so familiar? | It was built from songs like the ones she already plays | It was copied secretly from her best friend's playlist last night | It plays the same song again and again | It is chosen by a famous music teacher
test: "the shop app knows me better than me lol, should i get everything it says" Best reply? | It's made to sell; ask if you really need it | Yes, it knows exactly what you need | Yes, but only the cheapest things | No, it only suggests things nobody wants
test: A robocall says, "Press 1 and give your bank details to win a prize." What is it most likely to be? | A scam, so hang up | A real prize from a friendly robot | A survey from the government | A mistake, so press 1 to fix it
test: Which is the best example of AI making life fairer? | A phone reading a menu aloud for someone with weak eyes | A video site showing more of the same videos | A game giving surprise rewards just when you want to stop playing | A shop app suggesting more things to buy
test: The quiz says the capital of France is Rome. What can a pupil politely do? | Say, "I think this answer is wrong" | Write Rome, because the quiz says so | Stay quiet so nobody gets embarrassed | Skip all the other questions too
test: Grandad finds the photo app has put his photos in a "Grandad" album without asking him. What is sensible? | Ask a grown-up to look at the app's settings together | Throw the phone away at once | Take no photos of Grandad ever again | Nothing, apps never store faces anywhere, so there is nothing to look at
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the use of automated translation for important correspondence? | Such translation is of assistance but ought to be verified by a person proficient in both languages | Such translation is invariably accurate and accordingly requires no verification by any person | Such translation is permissible only for correspondence of trivial length | Such translation is prohibited for all official purposes
exam: It is given that automatically generated captions misrepresent a spoken word. Identify the persons for whom the error is of greatest consequence. | Viewers who are deaf and rely upon the captions | Viewers who are listening with the sound enabled | Persons who produced the original recording | Persons who do not view the recording at all
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the recommendation "persons similar to yourself also purchased". | It is designed principally to increase the sales of the retailer | It is designed principally to reduce the expenditure of the customer | It is compiled by an independent body for consumer protection | It identifies only those goods which are necessary
exam: An educator employs an automated tool in the preparation of a quiz, wherein one answer is subsequently found to be erroneous. Ascertain the inference which may properly be drawn. | Output of such tools requires verification even when used by adults | Such tools are incapable of producing any correct answer whatsoever in any subject | The erroneous answer must be accepted as correct | Educators are forbidden to employ such tools
